{"task_id": "HumanEval/26", "solution": "```python\ndef remove_duplicates(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a list of integers, remove all elements that occur more than once.\n    Keep order of elements left the same as in the input.\n    >>> remove_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 2, 4])\n    [1, 3, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    counts = {}\n    for num in numbers:\n        counts[num] = counts.get(num, 0) + 1\n\n    result_list = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        if counts[num] == 1:\n            result_list.append(num)\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Return list of prime factors of given integer in the order from smallest to largest.\n    Each of the factors should be listed number of times corresponding to how many times it appeares in factorization.\n    Input number should be equal to the product of all factors\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    def _handle_special_cases(n: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Handles edge cases for factorization (e.g., 0, 1, negative numbers).\"\"\"\n        # Prime factorization is typically defined for positive integers greater than 1.\n        if n <= 1:\n            return []\n        return None\n\n    def _get_prime_factors_logic(n: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Implements the core algorithm to find prime factors with multiplicity for positive integers greater than 1.\"\"\"\n        factors = []\n        temp_n = n\n\n        # Divide by 2 until it's no longer divisible\n        while temp_n % 2 == 0:\n            factors.append(2)\n            temp_n //= 2\n\n        # Check for odd factors starting from 3 up to sqrt(temp_n)\n        d = 3\n        while d * d <= temp_n:\n            while temp_n % d == 0:\n                factors.append(d)\n                temp_n //= d\n            d += 2\n\n        # If temp_n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself\n        if temp_n > 1:\n            factors.append(temp_n)\n\n        return factors\n\n    # Handle special cases first\n    special_case_result = _handle_special_cases(n)\n    if special_case_result is not None:\n        return special_case_result\n\n    # If not a special case, n must be a positive integer greater than 1\n    factors = _get_prime_factors_logic(n)\n\n    # The _get_prime_factors_logic function already produces factors in ascending order.\n    return factors\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/27", "solution": "```python\ndef flip_case(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" For a given string, flip lowercase characters to uppercase and uppercase to lowercase.\n    >>> flip_case('Hello')\n    'hELLO'\n    \"\"\"\n    result_chars = []\n    for char in string:\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':  # Check if lowercase alphabetic\n            result_chars.append(char.upper()) # Convert to uppercase\n        elif 'A' <= char <= 'Z': # Check if uppercase alphabetic\n            result_chars.append(char.lower()) # Convert to lowercase\n        else:\n            result_chars.append(char) # Retain non-alphabetic characters\n    return ''.join(result_chars)\n```"}
