{"task_id": "HumanEval/57", "solution": "```python\ndef monotonic(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return True is list elements are monotonically increasing or decreasing.\n    >>> monotonic([1, 2, 4, 20])\n    True\n    >>> monotonic([1, 20, 4, 10])\n    False\n    >>> monotonic([4, 1, 0, -10])\n    True\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l or len(l) == 1:\n        return True\n\n    is_increasing = True\n    is_decreasing = True\n\n    for i in range(1, len(l)):\n        if l[i] < l[i-1]:\n            is_increasing = False\n        if l[i] > l[i-1]:\n            is_decreasing = False\n\n    return is_increasing or is_decreasing\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/59", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_prime_factor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the largest prime factor of n. Assume n > 1 and is not a prime.\n    >>> largest_prime_factor(13195)\n    29\n    >>> largest_prime_factor(2048)\n    2\n    \"\"\"\n    i = 2\n    largest_factor = 1\n    temp_n = n\n\n    # Divide by 2 until n is odd\n    while temp_n % 2 == 0:\n        largest_factor = 2\n        temp_n //= 2\n\n    # Iterate for odd factors\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= temp_n:\n        while temp_n % i == 0:\n            largest_factor = i\n            temp_n //= i\n        i += 2\n\n    # If temp_n is still greater than 2, it must be a prime factor itself\n    if temp_n > 2:\n        largest_factor = temp_n\n\n    return largest_factor\n```"}
